  "data": {
    "title": "Topological Organization of Functional Brain Networks in Healthy Children: Differences in Relation to Age, Sex, and Intelligence",
    "text": "We measured functional connectivity by calculating the correlations between the time series of any pair of the 90 regions in the whole brain (defined by a prior atlas) during the resting state. A correlation matrix was obtained in each of 51 healthy children aged from 6 to 18 years and further thresholded into a binary, undirected network underlying the topological organization of a functional brain network.\n\nTo construct a functional brain network, we employed an automated anatomical labeling (AAL) atlas to parcellate the whole brain into 90 regions (45 in each hemisphere, N = 90). The mean time series of each region was then acquired by averaging the time series of all voxels within that region.\n\nThe functional connectivity between a pair of regions was defined as the Pearson’s correlation coefficient in the residual time courses. Thus, a functional connectivity matrix can be obtained for each subject.\n\nEach functional connectivity matrix can be converted to a binary, undirected network G using a cost threshold (0.05 ≤ t ≤ 0.5, step = 0.01).\n\nFive small-world parameters and two efficiency parameters were computed to characterize the global topological organization of the functional brain networks. Three regional nodal parameters were used to examine the properties of the 90 brain regions.\n\nAll network parameters were calculated using the Brain Connectivity Toolbox."
  },
